WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.860
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 96, it's July 8th, 2026.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.560
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:11.280
Let's go.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.640
You know how picking the right teammate for a task can make everything smoother.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.280
That idea sits at the center of today's biggest announcement.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:25.420
Open AI just revealed three new models heading to the public this Thursday, with preview access

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.900
rolling out to people worldwide right now.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.940
The models are called GPT 5.6 Soul, Terra and Luna.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.000
Each one brings its own working style even though they share the same core strengths.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.660
One model leans toward fast, back and forth exchanges that feel like a brainstorming session.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.580
Another prefers to receive a clear goal and then handle the full project without constant check-ins.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.720
Think of them like different tools in a kitchen drawer.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.840
A whisk works best for quick mixing while a slow cooker handles the long simmer on its own.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.240
This matters for anyone who uses AI for school essays, game ideas or creative writing.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.660
Soon you might choose one model when you want instant replies and switch to another

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.220
when the task needs steady, independent progress.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.480
The rollout starts with early access this week.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.560
If you want to see the differences yourself, keep an eye on the open AI website or app

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.160
for any preview sign up options they share.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:29.760
Trying the previews is the clearest way to notice how each model feels in real conversations.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.400
When you're texting with different friends, each one replies in their own style.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.220
One friend might send short, quick answers and keep the conversation bouncing back and forth.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.980
Another friend takes a little longer but comes back with a whole thoughtful paragraph

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.360
that moves the idea forward on its own.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.580
AI models are starting to work the same way.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.880
Some are built to stay right with you, reacting fast to every little change in what you say

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:01.360
so you can figure things out together. Others are designed to take a clear goal you give them

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.780
and then keep working steadily in the background without needing constant check-ins.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.600
The difference comes from how each model was trained on huge amounts of examples

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.340
and how its instructions guide it to either stay collaborative or push ahead independently.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.260
When you switch between them, it's like choosing which friend to brainstorm with

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.200
depending on whether you want fast back and forth energy or someone who will just handle the whole

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.120
thing once you explain what you need. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.100
The deep dive where we look under the hood at how these models end up with such different personalities.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.940
Picture a music school that trains every student on the same basic scales and chords first.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:48.320
After that foundation, some students get extra practice sessions focused on quick improvisation

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:54.020
with a partner. Other students spend their time learning to build long pieces alone from a single

00:02:54.020 --> 00:03:00.460
starting note. The shared scales are like the huge collection of text examples every model sees during

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:06.300
initial training. The extra practice sessions match the specific instructions and fine-tuning that

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.620
push one model toward constant collaboration and another toward independent follow-through.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:17.400
When you switch between the models later, you are really choosing which set of extra lessons you

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:23.860
want active for your task. That choice is what turns a single powerful system into several tools

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:30.160
that feel like distinct teammates. The result is that using AI starts to feel more like selecting

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:36.280
the right person for the job instead of hoping one tool fits everything. It is a small shift that

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:42.500
makes the technology feel more natural over time. Now let's move into some hands-on experiments you can

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:48.700
run today. First up is an update that turns an AI helper into something you can carry in your pocket.

